    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      How Did the Abolition of BSD and SSD Change Hong Kong Stamp Duty?
    </h2>
    <p>
      The abolition of cooling measures removed three major artificial friction points in the Hong Kong real estate market:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
      <li>
        <strong>Buyer's Stamp Duty (BSD) Abolished:</strong> Previously, non-Hong Kong permanent residents (including foreigners, mainland Chinese buyers, and corporate entities) paid an extra 7.5% (and earlier up to 15%) BSD surcharge. This is now completely abolished.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>New Residential Stamp Duty (NRSD) Abolished:</strong> The punitive 7.5% flat duty previously levied on buyers acquiring their second or subsequent residential property was eliminated. Existing homeowners can now buy additional properties without punitive surcharges.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Special Stamp Duty (SSD) Abolished:</strong> Homeowners were previously penalized with a 10% to 20% resale tax if they sold a property within 24 months of purchase. The elimination of SSD allows immediate resale without any punitive time-locked holding penalties.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      How Do You Calculate Ad Valorem Stamp Duty with Marginal Relief?
    </h2>
    <p>
      Marginal relief prevents sharp tax spikes at the threshold boundaries between rate bands. Taxpayers are charged the tax payable on the lower band plus a percentage (typically 10%) of the excess consideration above that band limit.
    </p>

    <!-- Marginal Relief Example Box -->
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 my-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-2 text-sm sm:text-base">
        Case Study: How Marginal Relief Saves Cash at HK$3.2 Million
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Suppose an expat purchases an apartment in Sha Tin for <strong>HK$3,200,000</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm space-y-1 text-muted dark:text-muted">
        <p><strong>Standard 1.5% Rate:</strong> HK$3,200,000 &times; 1.5% = <strong>HK$48,000</strong>.</p>
        <p><strong>Marginal Relief Formula:</strong> HK$100 + 10% &times; (HK$3,200,000 &minus; HK$3,000,000) = HK$100 + HK$20,000 = <strong>HK$20,100</strong>.</p>
        <p class="font-bold text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">
          The buyer pays only HK$20,100—saving HK$27,900 in upfront stamp duty!
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      When is Property Stamp Duty Payable to the Inland Revenue Department?
    </h2>
    <p>
      Under the Stamp Duty Ordinance, stamp duty on residential property transactions must be paid to the Stamp Office of the Inland Revenue Department within 30 days of executing the formal Sale and Purchase Agreement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Failing to stamp property instruments within the statutory 30-day window triggers punitive late stamping surcharges under Section 9 of the Stamp Duty Ordinance:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Delay up to 1 month:</strong> Penalty of <strong>2 times</strong> the stamp duty amount.</li>
      <li><strong>Delay between 1 and 2 months:</strong> Penalty of <strong>4 times</strong> the stamp duty amount.</li>
      <li><strong>Delay exceeding 2 months:</strong> Maximum penalty of <strong>10 times</strong> the stamp duty amount.</li>
    </ul>

